// Leaving a blank.
//
// A definition can leave the type of something to be said later. The blank is a name YOU choose —
// there are no single-letter conventions here, and nothing to import. For an object, `of` marks it:
// the slot after the type's own name. For a function there is no such slot, so its signature
// introduces the blank instead — a name that names no type, used at least twice.
//
// The thing worth noticing: a filling is not a box. `a stack of number` and `a stack of text` are
// two separate types with two separate bodies, worked out before the program runs. That is why
// `the first of names's items` below is a text, and why nothing has to be unwrapped or checked at
// runtime to get it.

// ── An object with one blank ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Define object stack of element with (the series of element items):
    Bind void to push, given (the element value):
        Insert value into one's items.
    Done.

    Bind number to how-many:
        Return the number of one's items.
    Done.
Done.

Define counts as a new stack of number { the items a series of number }.
Cast push on (counts, 5).
Cast push on (counts, 7).

Define names as a new stack of text { the items a series of text }.
Cast push on (names, "Ada").
Cast push on (names, "Grace").

State "Two fillings of one definition:".
State "  stack of number holds {cast how-many on counts} — first is {(the first of counts's items) converted to text}".
State "  stack of text   holds {cast how-many on names} — first is {the first of names's items}".

// ── An object with two blanks, because you name each ───────────────────────────────────────────

Define object pair of left-thing of right-thing with (
    the left-thing one-side,
    the right-thing other-side
).

Define labelled as a new pair of number of text { the one-side 7, the other-side "seven" }.

State "".
State "Two blanks, filled in order:".
State "  {labelled's one-side converted to text} is spelled {labelled's other-side}".

// ── A function with a blank ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Nothing declares `element` here. The signature uses it twice — once in what goes in, once in what
// comes out — and that is what makes it a blank rather than a misspelled type. Used only once it
// would stay an error, which is the point: a typo should not quietly become a generic function.

Bind series of element to first-two, given (the series of element xs):
    Define out as a series of element.
    Insert the first of xs into out.
    Insert item 2 of xs into out.
    Return out.
Done.

// The blank may sit inside another type — this is the shape `minimum` and `maximum` have.
Bind voidable element to first-or-none, given (the series of element xs):
    If the number of xs is 0:
        Return void.
    Done.
    Return the first of xs.
Done.

Define numbers as a series of number with (3, 6, 9).
Define words as a series of text with ("red", "green", "blue").
Define nothing-here as a series of text.

State "".
State "One function, filled from whatever it is handed:".
State "  first two numbers: {the number of (cast first-two on (numbers)) converted to text} of them, starting {(the first of (cast first-two on (numbers))) converted to text}".
State "  first two words:   {the first of (cast first-two on (words))}, {item 2 of (cast first-two on (words))}".

State "".
State "A blank inside a voidable:".
Define found as (cast first-or-none on (numbers)) but void is 0.
Define missing as (cast first-or-none on (nothing-here)) but void is "(empty)".
State "  first of numbers: {found converted to text}".
State "  first of nothing: {missing}".
